Well, remember, these are people who changed their mind when Stalin was still in power.

218
01:52:14,000 --> 01:52:16,000
So that's the key.

219
01:52:16,000 --> 01:52:24,000
So once I actually got an email from a communist, I did a lot of work in grad school on the atrocities of communism.

220
01:52:24,000 --> 01:52:27,000
He said, look, fine, there were a lot of atrocities.

221
01:52:27,000 --> 01:52:34,000
Those all fixed in 1956 when Khrushchev went and said, we're really, really sorry to some of you.

222
01:52:34,000 --> 01:52:37,000
Especially people who were in the Communist Party.

223
01:52:37,000 --> 01:52:42,000
That was the real tragedy is when Communist Party members suffered.

224
01:52:42,000 --> 01:52:46,000
That's the worst thing that could happen is for a loyal communist to suffer, right?

225
01:52:46,000 --> 01:52:47,000
Right?

226
01:52:47,000 --> 01:52:48,000
All right.

227
01:52:48,000 --> 01:52:56,000
Now, something else you can pile on is to say, look, rational thinking is often hard, painful, discouraging work.

228
01:52:56,000 --> 01:53:01,000
So you ever work on a math problem for an hour and you get it wrong a bunch of times and finally you get something that kind of looks like the right answer?

229
01:53:01,000 --> 01:53:03,000
Then you go, yep, that's the answer.

230
01:53:03,000 --> 01:53:04,000
I'm done.

231
01:53:04,000 --> 01:53:05,000
Right?

232
01:53:05,000 --> 01:53:08,000
Now, reasonably, you have to say, well, given that I messed it up a bunch of other times,

233
01:53:08,000 --> 01:53:13,000
maybe I worked on this for one more hour, I would find that this kind of answer is wrong, too.

234
01:53:13,000 --> 01:53:20,000
But it's just so discouraging to do this kind of thing that a lot of people would just preemptively say, I've got it.

235
01:53:20,000 --> 01:53:21,000
Solved.

236
01:53:21,000 --> 01:53:22,000
Recognition accomplished.

237
01:53:22,000 --> 01:53:25,000
I've got the answer.

238
01:53:25,000 --> 01:53:30,000
Just the whole mindset of what you have to do in order to become accurate.

239
01:53:30,000 --> 01:53:32,000
So, again, I talk a lot about Phil Tetlock.

240
01:53:32,000 --> 01:53:38,000
He's someone who actually has a lot of great empirical work on what are things you do if you want to become accurate.

241
01:53:38,000 --> 01:53:41,000
A lot of it is pretty simple.

242
01:53:41,000 --> 01:53:43,000
Again, most of it is common sense.

243
01:53:43,000 --> 01:53:47,000
Don't say things are impossible or certain unless they're like 1 plus 1 equals 2.

244
01:53:47,000 --> 01:53:50,000
So for any interesting question, stop saying it's certain or impossible.

245
01:53:50,000 --> 01:53:52,000
All right?

246
01:53:52,000 --> 01:53:54,000
And here's another one.

247
01:53:54,000 --> 01:53:58,000
Say what would count as you being right or wrong before the events pass.

248
01:53:58,000 --> 01:54:03,000
Try to be as unambiguous as possible.

249
01:54:03,000 --> 01:54:09,000
So you then aren't tempted to go and wait for whatever happens and say, oh, just as I predicted, once again.

250
01:54:09,000 --> 01:54:11,000
All right?

251
01:54:11,000 --> 01:54:13,000
So you didn't say what would count as winning.

252
01:54:13,000 --> 01:54:17,000
You waited to see what happened and then did what Tetlock calls self-scoring.

253
01:54:17,000 --> 01:54:24,000
Self-scoring where you go and referee your own match and say, wow, I win.

254
01:54:24,000 --> 01:54:25,000
I'm the winner.

255
01:54:25,000 --> 01:54:26,000
I'm also the player.

256
01:54:26,000 --> 01:54:29,000
But I'm the player, I'm the referee, and I'm the winner.

257
01:54:29,000 --> 01:54:33,000
Wow, that's amazing that you won when you are the referee.

258
01:54:33,000 --> 01:54:34,000
Big surprise.

259
01:54:34,000 --> 01:54:36,000
So what is a good thing to do?

260
01:54:36,000 --> 01:54:41,000
Well, minimum, like publicly say, if the unemployment rate goes above 8.5 percent, then I am wrong.

261
01:54:41,000 --> 01:54:44,000
Otherwise, I am right.

262
01:54:44,000 --> 01:54:50,000
Even that's not as good as actually betting with someone who disagrees with you because when you go and say that 8.5,

263
01:54:50,000 --> 01:54:55,000
it may make the goalposts so easy for yourself to pass.

264
01:54:55,000 --> 01:55:02,000
So I have a Keynesian friend who said, look, the fact that inflation stayed low during the last recession shows Keynesianism is right.

265
01:55:02,000 --> 01:55:08,000
And I said, yeah, but I'm not, at least you're kind of Keynesian and I predicted exactly the same thing.

266
01:55:08,000 --> 01:55:10,000
So how does that show you're right?

267
01:55:10,000 --> 01:55:15,000
Again, the right thing for him to do, what it would have been to have done, would have been to find a person that he disagreed with

268
01:55:15,000 --> 01:55:17,000
and work out a list of disagreements.

269
01:55:17,000 --> 01:55:25,000
And then say, we have hammered out a deal where two people, both of whom noticeably disagree,

270
01:55:25,000 --> 01:55:29,000
have worked out a list under which only one will be right in the end.

271
01:55:29,000 --> 01:55:33,000
And we are now publicizing for credibility.

272
01:55:33,000 --> 01:55:37,000
Of course, there's a big downside to doing that kind of thing.

273
01:55:37,000 --> 01:55:40,000
Egg on your face.

274
01:55:40,000 --> 01:55:43,000
If you do anything that would allow you to be unambiguously right,

275
01:55:43,000 --> 01:55:47,000
it will also automatically allow you to be unambiguously wrong.

276
01:55:47,000 --> 01:55:49,000
People don't like that.

277
01:55:49,000 --> 01:55:54,000
And then, again, very basic things like don't be a single theory to explain everything person.

278
01:55:54,000 --> 01:55:57,000
Admit that there's a lot of theories, all of which have sub-use.

279
01:55:57,000 --> 01:56:01,000
There's a bunch of words that you can just drop from your vocabulary if you want to get better.

280
01:56:01,000 --> 01:56:03,000
Drop the word inherently.

281
01:56:03,000 --> 01:56:06,000
Drop the word necessarily.

282
01:56:06,000 --> 01:56:09,000
These are terrible words if you want to go and predict anything.

283
01:56:09,000 --> 01:56:13,000
But remember, as you were saying, that necessarily, look, I don't say it's necessary.

284
01:56:13,000 --> 01:56:15,000
I say it's going to happen.

285
01:56:15,000 --> 01:56:17,000
I don't say it's necessary.

286
01:56:17,000 --> 01:56:20,000
I don't know of any way of empirically checking whether something's necessary.

287
01:56:20,000 --> 01:56:23,000
So let's not bet on that.

288
01:56:23,000 --> 01:56:26,000
Let's bet on something that we can see.

289
01:56:26,000 --> 01:56:29,000
And then, an indirect reason is also important.

290
01:56:29,000 --> 01:56:32,000
The way that other people treat you may depend upon your beliefs.

291
01:56:32,000 --> 01:56:35,000
So right now, suppose that you were to announce that you know that you disagree

292
01:56:35,000 --> 01:56:38,000
with all of your current controversial political beliefs.

293
01:56:38,000 --> 01:56:40,000
How many friends would you lose?

294
01:56:40,000 --> 01:56:45,000
You go on Facebook and you say, I used to believe the following ten things.

295
01:56:45,000 --> 01:56:48,000
I now believe none of them.

296
01:56:48,000 --> 01:56:50,000
And first, most people usually think it's a joke, right?

297
01:56:50,000 --> 01:56:52,000
Check to see if it's April Fool's.

298
01:56:52,000 --> 01:56:54,000
And then you go and say, look, and here's why.

299
01:56:54,000 --> 01:56:57,000
How many friends are you likely to lose?

300
01:56:57,000 --> 01:57:02,000
Of course, no one here would have kind of low friends that would get mad at you just for

301
01:57:02,000 --> 01:57:04,000
irrationally updating reviews.

302
01:57:04,000 --> 01:57:08,000
But other people have bad friends that will get angry just because they change their minds

303
01:57:08,000 --> 01:57:13,000
and won't give you the benefit of the doubt and won't say, since we are friends, I know

304
01:57:13,000 --> 01:57:17,000
you're well-meaning and a reasonable person, so please share your arguments with me.

305
01:57:17,000 --> 01:57:20,000
But a lot of people don't have friends like that.

306
01:57:20,000 --> 01:57:25,000
They're stuck with regular human friends.

307
01:57:25,000 --> 01:57:27,000
Now, what is the price of rationality?

308
01:57:27,000 --> 01:57:29,000
So at first glance, it may be hard.

309
01:57:29,000 --> 01:57:32,000
But then you realize a lot of things in economics, we talk about the price,

310
01:57:32,000 --> 01:57:35,000
there is an invisible price like the price of safety on the job.

311
01:57:35,000 --> 01:57:38,000
You don't actually go to your employer and say, I'm going to pay you something to go

312
01:57:38,000 --> 01:57:40,000
and make the job safer.

313
01:57:40,000 --> 01:57:46,000
Instead, what do you actually do in the labor market for what workers actually do?

314
01:57:46,000 --> 01:57:54,000
Like how do we actually work out safety, regulation aside, but besides that, how do we work it out?

315
01:57:54,000 --> 01:57:55,000
Tacitly.

316
01:57:55,000 --> 01:57:56,000
Work it out tacitly.

317
01:57:56,000 --> 01:57:58,000
Yes, so, yes, you have compensating differentials.

318
01:57:58,000 --> 01:58:02,000
So employers know, look, if the job is dangerous, I'm going to need to offer more money.

319
01:58:02,000 --> 01:58:05,000
I can go and make it safer, and then I can offer less money.

320
01:58:05,000 --> 01:58:10,000
Or similarly, workers could say, look, I'll work here, like I'll work under these conditions,

321
01:58:10,000 --> 01:58:14,000
so I'm not going to work under those conditions, and I'm not going to have to pay any more.

322
01:58:14,000 --> 01:58:15,000
All right.

323
01:58:15,000 --> 01:58:18,000
So same way you think about the price of rationality.

324
01:58:18,000 --> 01:58:20,000
Here is a price.

325
01:58:20,000 --> 01:58:26,000
We think about it as the dollar value of the bad material consequences you have as a result

326
01:58:26,000 --> 01:58:37,000
of being irrational, and the quantity, the amount of deviation from rational expectations,

327
01:58:37,000 --> 01:58:41,000
and the spa-regular old demand curve.

328
01:58:41,000 --> 01:58:45,000
So when the price of rationality is high, which it often is, like if you're a doctor

329
01:58:45,000 --> 01:58:49,000
and you believe I'm such a great doctor, I'm better drunk than other doctors are sober,

330
01:58:49,000 --> 01:58:51,000
costly belief, right?

331
01:58:51,000 --> 01:58:52,000
Like they destroy your career.

332
01:58:52,000 --> 01:58:57,000
So the price is high, and certain people at least consume less, maybe not at all.

